# Wick — Screens, menus, and audio

This file defines the screens Wick opens on and plays on, what each shows, how a
player moves between them, and the game's audio. The rules the screens present
are in `specs/world.md`, `specs/progression.md`, and `specs/evolutions.md`; the
actions and the pointer the menus are driven by are in `specs/controls.md`; and
the icons, sprites, and sounds the screens draw and play are the produced files
`specs/assets.md` lists. Every figure and every piece of screen copy below
carries the name this specification gives it.

## Presentation

Wick fixes no palette, no font, and no styling for any screen, and each
screen's layout is yours except where a table below places one element relative
to another. The look of the night is yours, and `specs/overview.md` states what
a player reads at a glance in the world. Two requirements are this file's own.
Every piece of text a screen shows is legible against whatever sits behind it at
the logical stage size `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`), the HUD included,
which is drawn over the live world. On every menu the item at `menuIndex` is
drawn distinctly from the others, so a player always sees which item `confirm`
would accept.

## Screens

`screen` names the screen the game is in.

| Screen | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `title` | The front door: the title, the tagline, and the main menu. |
| `howto` | How the game is played, in a player's words. |
| `almanac` | Everything the night holds, listed and described. |
| `playing` | The night: the world, the lamplighter, and the HUD. The only screen that ticks. |
| `levelup` | An overlay over the held world, offering a level-up's choices. |
| `chest` | An overlay over the held world, showing what a chest did. |
| `paused` | The world held still under `PAUSED_TEXT`. |
| `fallen` | The run's end when health ran out. |
| `dawn` | The run's end when the clock reached dawn. |

### `title`

The game opens here, and the debug surface's `reset` returns here.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `TITLE_TEXT` | `WICK` |
| Tagline | `TAGLINE_TEXT` | `KEEP THE LIGHT` |
| Menu | `TITLE_ITEMS` | `LIGHT THE LAMP`, `THE ALMANAC`, `HOW TO PLAY`, in that order |

The menu's items are stacked one above the next under the title and tagline.
`menuIndex` is `0` on arriving. `up` and `down` move the highlight by one item
and wrap at both ends, `confirm` takes the highlighted item, and `back` does
nothing.

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `LIGHT THE LAMP` | Starts a fresh run, defined below, and sets `screen = playing`. |
| `THE ALMANAC` | Sets `screen = almanac`, with `menuIndex`, `almanacTab`, and `almanacScroll` all `0`. |
| `HOW TO PLAY` | Sets `screen = howto` and `menuIndex = 0`. |

The night may show behind the menu, dimmed or otherwise quieted.

### `howto`

How to play, written in a player's words rather than as the rules of a system.
It covers:

- that the lamplighter only moves, and that every tool fires on its own, each
  on its own rhythm;
- that everything in the dark is drawn to the light and hurts on touch;
- that what falls leaves gems, that gems raise the lamp's level, and that each
  level offers a choice of one from three;
- that there are six tool slots and six trinket slots;
- that the big ones drop chests, and that a chest transforms a tool at its top
  level when the lamplighter carries its trinket;
- that the night ends at dawn, `10:00` on the clock, and that reaching it is
  the win;
- that the almanac, opened from the title, lists every tool, trinket, enemy,
  and pickup the night holds;
- the controls, naming the keys `BINDINGS` gives each action: the arrows or
  `WASD` to move, `Enter` or `Space` to confirm, `Escape` to go back or to
  pause, `P` to pause, and `M` to mute, and that every menu answers the mouse
  as well.

`back` returns to `title` with `menuIndex = 0`.

### `almanac`

Everything the night holds, browsed at rest. It shows `ALMANAC_TEXT`
(`THE ALMANAC`), the tab bar `ALMANAC_TABS` (`TOOLS`, `TRINKETS`, `ENEMIES`,
`PICKUPS`, in that order) across the top with the tab at `almanacTab` drawn
distinctly, the entries of that tab listed down the left with the entry at
`menuIndex` drawn distinctly, and that entry shown in full to the right of the
list.

| Tab | Entries, in this order |
| --- | --- |
| `TOOLS` | the ten of `BASE_WEAPON_IDS`, then the six of `EVOLUTION_IDS` |
| `TRINKETS` | the ten of `PASSIVE_IDS` |
| `ENEMIES` | the thirteen of `ENEMY_IDS` |
| `PICKUPS` | the three of `GEM_TIERS`, then the three of `PICKUP_KINDS` |

The list shows `ALMANAC_ROWS` (`10`) entries at a time, beginning at the entry
at `almanacScroll`, and a tab of `ALMANAC_ROWS` entries or fewer shows all of
them. Each row shows its entry's name.

The entry at `menuIndex` is shown in four parts:

| Part | Content |
| --- | --- |
| Name | The weapon's from `WEAPON_NAMES`, the passive's from `PASSIVES`, the enemy's from `ENEMIES`, or the gem's or the pickup's from the names below. |
| Picture | The produced sprite the table below names, animated where that sprite is a sheet. |
| Stats | The lines the table below names, each the label written exactly as it appears there and its figure beside it. |
| Description | The entry's line from the Descriptions section below, on one line. |

| Tab | Picture | Stats |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TOOLS` | the weapon's icon and its effect, animated where the effect is a sheet | `DAMAGE` and the `damage` of its level `1` row, and `COOLDOWN` and that row's `cooldown` in seconds; an evolved weapon's fixed row is that row, and Chandelier, whose fixed row carries no cooldown, shows `DAMAGE` alone |
| `TRINKETS` | the passive's icon | `MAX LEVEL` and its max level in `PASSIVES` |
| `ENEMIES` | the enemy's walk sheet, animated at `WALK_FRAME_TIME` | `HEALTH`, `SPEED`, and `DAMAGE`, and the `hp`, `speed`, and `damage` of its row in `ENEMIES` |
| `PICKUPS` | the gem's or the pickup's sprite | a gem: `EXPERIENCE` and its tier's value in `GEM_VALUES`; `bread`: `HEALS` and `BREAD_HEAL` (`30`); `chest` and `draft`: none |

The gems and the pickups carry the display names `GEM_NAMES` and
`PICKUP_NAMES` give them:

| Constant | Id | Name |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `GEM_NAMES` | `small` | `Small Gem` |
| `GEM_NAMES` | `medium` | `Medium Gem` |
| `GEM_NAMES` | `large` | `Large Gem` |
| `PICKUP_NAMES` | `chest` | `Chest` |
| `PICKUP_NAMES` | `bread` | `Bread` |
| `PICKUP_NAMES` | `draft` | `Draft` |

`menuIndex`, `almanacTab`, and `almanacScroll` are `0` on arriving. `up` and
`down` move `menuIndex` by one over the tab's entries and wrap at both ends.
`left` and `right` move `almanacTab` by one over `ALMANAC_TABS`, wrap at both
ends, and set `menuIndex` and `almanacScroll` to `0`. `back` returns to `title`
with `menuIndex`, `almanacTab`, and `almanacScroll` all `0`. `confirm` and
`pause` do nothing here.

`almanacScroll` follows the highlight: after every move of `menuIndex` it
becomes the lesser of `almanacScroll` and `menuIndex`, then the greater of that
and `menuIndex − ALMANAC_ROWS + 1`, and is then held between `0` and
`max(0, count − ALMANAC_ROWS)`, for `count` the number of entries the tab
holds.

The almanac holds the idle run, nothing advances while it is open, and no cue
loops on it.

### `playing`

The live night, and the only screen the simulation ticks on. It shows the
world through the view, the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` rectangle centered on the
lamplighter, with the ground pattern repeating in world space so motion reads,
and every enemy, projectile, zone, gem, and pickup inside the view drawn at its
world position. The lamplighter is drawn at the stage center
`(STAGE_CX, STAGE_CY)` (`640, 360`), facing the way `facing` says.

The HUD is drawn over the world and reads against it:

| Element | What the player sees |
| --- | --- |
| Health | A bar filled from its left edge, its filled width `hp / maxHp` of the bar's width, with both numbers beside it, `hp` rounded up to a whole number. |
| Experience | A bar filled from its left edge, its filled width `xp / xpToNext` of the bar's width, labeled with `LEVEL_LABEL` (`LEVEL`) and the current level, as `LEVEL 4`. |
| Clock | The run clock as `m:ss`, counting up from `0:00` in whole seconds, the seconds always two digits. |
| Kills | The kill count. |
| Weapon slots | `WEAPON_SLOTS` (`6`) slots in slot order, each held weapon as its icon with one pip per level held, the pips in a row outside the square the icon is drawn in and the only marks that row gains over a slot holding nothing, and an empty slot visibly empty, drawing neither icon nor pip. |
| Passive slots | `PASSIVE_SLOTS` (`6`) slots, the same way. |
| Cooldowns | On each weapon's slot, its cooldown state: the slot's picture on the tick the weapon fires differs from its picture on the tick before, and a weapon whose timer is `0` shows nothing left to run, its slot drawing the same picture on every tick the timer stays `0`. A Lantern slot's timer covers the set's life and the gap after it together. |
| Hurt | While `hurtFlash` is above `0`, a hurt cast over the view, so the stage drawn on a tick with `hurtFlash` above `0` differs from the stage drawn on the same state with `hurtFlash` at `0`. Its color, its shape, and its fade are yours. |

### `levelup`

An overlay over the world, which stays drawn beneath it exactly as the tick
that opened the overlay left it. It shows `LEVEL_UP_TEXT`
(`THE LAMP BURNS BRIGHTER`) and the offers in `offers`, listed vertically in
that order. Each offer shows three things:

| Part | Content |
| --- | --- |
| Icon | The item's produced icon. |
| Name | The weapon's name from `WEAPON_NAMES`, the passive's from `PASSIVES`, or `LAMP_OIL_NAME` (`Lamp Oil`). |
| Tag | `OFFER_NEW_TEXT` (`NEW`) for an item not yet held, else `LEVEL_LABEL` and the level it would become, as `LEVEL 3`. Lamp oil is never held, so its tag is `NEW`. |

Beneath the offer list the overlay draws one more line: the description of the
offer at `menuIndex`, on one line. A weapon's is its line in
`WEAPON_DESCRIPTIONS`, a passive's is its line in `PASSIVE_DESCRIPTIONS`, and
lamp oil's is `LAMP_OIL_DESCRIPTION`, all of them below.

`menuIndex` is `0` on opening. `up` and `down` move the highlight and wrap at
both ends, and `confirm` accepts the highlighted offer, as
`specs/progression.md` states. Then, if another level-up is queued, the next
overlay opens with a fresh set of offers and `menuIndex = 0`; else
`screen = playing` and the simulation resumes on the next tick. `back` and
`pause` do nothing here.

### `chest`

An overlay over the held world, opened as `specs/progression.md` states. It
shows `CHEST_TEXT` (`A CHEST OPENS`) and the result in `chestResult`:

| `kind` | Shows |
| --- | --- |
| `evolve` | The evolved weapon's icon and its name from `WEAPON_NAMES`. |
| `level` | The item's icon and name, and `LEVEL_LABEL` with the level it became. |
| `heal` | That the lamplighter was healed `CHEST_HEAL` (`30`), in words of your own. |

`confirm` closes the overlay, sounding no cue: `screen = playing`, and the
simulation resumes on the next tick. `up`, `down`, `back`, and `pause` do
nothing here.

### `paused`

The world held still, with the HUD, under `PAUSED_TEXT` (`PAUSED`), and the
menu `PAUSE_ITEMS` below it: `RESUME`, `MAIN MENU`, in that order.

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `RESUME` | Sets `screen = playing`, with the run untouched. |
| `MAIN MENU` | Abandons the run and returns to `title` with `menuIndex = 0`. |

`menuIndex` is `0` on arriving. `up` and `down` move the highlight and wrap at
both ends, and `confirm` takes the highlighted item. `pause` and `back` both
return to `playing` with the run untouched, sounding no cue.

### `fallen` and `dawn`

The two ends of a run. `fallen` shows `FALLEN_TEXT` (`THE LIGHT WENT OUT`) and
`dawn` shows `DAWN_TEXT` (`DAWN`); each shows the run's figures and the same
menu below them. The world may stay drawn beneath.

| Element | Content |
| --- | --- |
| Time survived | The run clock at the end, as `m:ss`. |
| Level | The level reached. |
| Kills | The kill count. |
| Menu | `END_ITEMS`: `TRY AGAIN`, `TITLE`, in that order. |

`menuIndex` is `0` on arriving. `up` and `down` move the highlight and wrap,
and `confirm` takes the highlighted item: `TRY AGAIN` starts a fresh run and
sets `screen = playing`, and `TITLE` returns to `title` with `menuIndex = 0`.
`back` does what `TITLE` does.

